{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To put into a bag.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} {{n-g|To take with oneself, to assume into one's score}}
## {{lb|en|informal}} To [[catch]] or [[kill]], especially when [[fishing]] or [[hunting]].
##: {{ux|en|We '''bagged''' three deer yesterday.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|title=[[:s:Sikhim and Bhutan|Sikhim and Bhutan]]|author=John Claude White|year=1909|page=55
|passage=He was a fine specimen, very large and with a beautiful coat, and I wish I had had the luck to bag him.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1936|year_published=1940|author=F.J. Thwaites|authorlink=F.J. Thwaites|title={{w|The Redemption (novel)|The Redemption}}|publisher=H. John Edwards|location=Sydney|chapter=XIV|page=147|text="As a matter of fact my thoughts were flashing between Ronda and that man-eating tiger I'm going to '''bag''' tomorrow."}}
## To gain [[possession]] of something, or to make first [[claim]] on something.
## {{lb|en|slang}} To [[steal]].
##* '''1950''', C. S. Lewis, ''The Lion, the Witch and the Wardrobe''
##*: "I am sure nobody would mind," said Susan. "It isn't as if we wanted to take them out of the house; we shan't take them even out of the wardrobe."<br>"I never thought of that, Su," said Peter. "Of course, now you put it that way, I see. No one could say you had '''bagged''' a coat as long as you leave it in the wardrobe where you found it. And I suppose this whole country is in the wardrobe."
## {{lb|en|slang|AAVE}} To take a [[woman]] away with one as a romantic or sexual interest.
##* {{quote-song|en|year=2020|artist=Eminem ft. Ed Sheeran|title=Those Kinda Nights|album=Music to Be Murdered By|passage=When we hit the club to go and hell-raise / Probably end up '''baggin' ''' the cocktail waitress}}
## {{lb|en|slang}} To [[arrest]].
##: {{syn|en|nick}}
##* {{quote-song|en|author=JS x Jtrapz|title=Straight On Smoke|date=29-01-2021|passage=Free bro, free bro, we got '''bagged''' for a M|note=0:54–0:56}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[furnish]] or [[load]] with a bag.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Don Sebastian}}
#*: a bee '''bagged''' with his honeyed venom
## {{lb|en|transitive|medicine}} To provide with artificial [[ventilation]] via a [[bag valve mask]] (BVM) [[resuscitator]].
## {{lb|en|transitive|medicine}} To fit with a bag to collect [[urine]].
##* '''1985''', Sol S. Zimmerman, Joan Holter Gildea, ''Critical Care Pediatrics'' (page 205)
##*: The patient was '''bagged''' for a urine analysis and stat electrolytes were drawn.
# {{n-g|To expose exterior shape or physical behaviour resembling that of a bag}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|ambitransitive}} To (cause to) [[swell]] or hang down like a full bag.
##: {{ux|en|The skin '''bags''' from containing morbid matter.}}
##: {{ux|en|The brisk wind '''bagged''' the sails.}}
## To hang like an [[empty]] bag.
##* {{RQ:Orwell Burmese Days|[http://www.gutenberg.net.au/ebooks02/0200051h.html Chapter 3]|[...] he was dressed in a badly fitting white drill suit, with trousers '''bagging''' concertina-like over clumsy black boots.}}
##* '''2004''', {{w|Andrea Levy}}, ''{{w|Small Island (novel)|Small Island}}'', London: Review, Chapter Eleven, p. 125,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/smallisland00levy]</sup>
##*: And this uniform did not even fit me so well. But what is a little '''bagging''' on the waist and tightness under the arm when you are a gallant member of the British Royal Air Force?
##: {{ux|en|His trousers '''bag''' at the knees.}}
## {{lb|en|nautical|intransitive}} To drop away from the correct [[course]].
## {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To become [[pregnant]].
##* {{R:Warner Albion}}
##*: Venus shortly '''bagged''', and ere long was Cupid bread
# To [[forget]], [[ignore]], or [[get rid of]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1977| title=The Publication of Poetry and Fiction|author=| page=97| passage=I may just '''bag''' that. I think poets have an obligation to boost the magazines they appear in.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998| title=Precision Heart Rate Training |author=Ed Burke | page=78| passage=Well, even if your VCR is still blinking “12:00," I hope you're smart enough to stay inside when it's that cold and just '''bag''' that workout.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1999| title105-1 Hearing: Implementation of Fast Track Trade Authority|author=United States. Congress. House. Committee on Ways and Means | page=| passage=I will just '''bag''' that. If not in the trade bill, that people believe should not interfere with the President's ability to negotiate a trade agreement, how would it be dealt with? }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2002| title=Time's Fool: A Tale in Verse |author=Glyn Maxwell | page=296| passage='Oh '''bag''' that,' said Nelson. 'Do the Edmund stuff — no, cut,  we'll do it later, look, it's knocking midnight.'}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007| title=Ripple Effect |author=Don Pendleton | page=322| passage=“Or we can '''bag''' that part of it and just go straight inside,” Bolan suggested. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014| title=Spider Kiss|author=Harlan Ellison | page=| passage=I'll get the sonofa—” “Listen, just '''bag''' that punchout shit for the moment. You've got a problem, and don't forget it. }}
# {{n-g|To show particular [[puffy]] emotion}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|intransitive}} To swell with [[arrogance]].
##: {{rfquotek|en|Chaucer}}
## {{lb|en|slang|African American Vernacular}} To [[laugh]] uncontrollably.
## {{lb|en|Australia|slang}} To [[criticise]] sarcastically.

